- The target can't be `xml` in any letter case; that name is reserved for the XML declaration. Writing such a node throws an `XmlException` ("Processing instruction target cannot be 'xml'."). For the declaration, use `XmlDeclarationNode` or let the writer add it.
- The constructor rejects a `null`, empty, or white-space target.
- `XmlNode.Parse` keeps processing instructions inside the root element. Those before and after the root element, such as `<?xml-stylesheet ...?>`, aren't part of the tree; `XmlDocument` keeps them and writes them back.
- A processing instruction doesn't contribute to `XmlElementNode.InnerText`.
